      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Mechanism Differences"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "ACE Inhibitors: Stop the enzyme that produces angiotensin II and inadvertently lead to a build-up of bradykinin, which triggers the characteristic dry cough and rare risk of swelling () in some people.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "ARBs: Let angiotensin II be formed but prevent it from binding to its target receptors. Because they leave bradykinin alone, they spare patients the nagging cough.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              3
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Clinical Guidance & Selection"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "First-Line Status: Major guidelines (such as ACC/AHA and ESC/ESH) list ACE inhibitors, , thiazide diuretics, and calcium channel blockers (CCBs) as interchangeable first-line options.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              4,
              7
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Why ARBs are Gaining Ground: Recent clinical shifts lean toward starting with ARBs primarily because patients tolerate them much better, leading to higher long-term adherence.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "When ACE Inhibitors Are Still Chosen: ACE inhibitors have a massive legacy of clinical trial data in specific settings (such as post-myocardial infarction or certain chronic kidney disease profiles), and cost or insurance formulary preferences sometimes make them the initial pick.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              5
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Crucial Rule: Never combine an ACE inhibitor and an ARB together, as dual blockade increases the risk of dangerously high potassium () and worsening kidney function without added clinical benefit.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              8
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
